            "scenarioName": "Vendor Access Lifecycle",
            "recommendedDifficulty": "Normal",
            "headline": "Order the support portal events from commissioning to the most recent capture.",
            "items": [
              "Historian commissioned and support portal opened to BridgeWorks technicians",
              "Second BridgeWorks technician account added to the portal",
              "BridgeWorks files for dissolution and posts a final invoice notice",
              "Portal marked read-only pending an internal migration",
              "Contact page removed and the portal delisted from the sitemap",
              "Final capture shows the portal still live with both author profiles listed"
            ],
            "lesson": "Legacy content and author metadata persist unless somebody owns their removal."
          }
        ],
        "checksAllowed": 3
      },
      "persona": "Whitehat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Dana Osei:</b> The portal kept an archive of its own snapshots, and every capture carries a date. They are filed by capture number rather than chronologically, so the order on the page means nothing.\n\nOnce the events sit in sequence I have a timeline for legal instead of an assertion, and a timeline is the only thing that will move this.",
      "taskInstructions": "Every slot has to be filled before CHECK ORDER will grade the board. Correct positions lock in place, wrong ones drop back to the pool, and the third failed check locks the console out.",
      "taskQuestion": "Reconstruct the BridgeWorks portal timeline.",
      "taskCompleteText": "<b>Dana Osei:</b> The timeline shows the portal and both author profiles remained published after the dissolution notice. It does not establish authentication status."
    },
